<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="ws_4_1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Practice Worksheet</title>

Simplify the following so that positive exponents remain



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>(x^2y^5)^0</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This expression equals <m>1</m>, since anything to the power <m>0</m> is just <m>1</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\pbr{\dfrac{x^{-3}}{xy^2}}^{-1}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>

<p>
<md>
<mrow>\pbr{\dfrac{x^{-3}}{xy^2}}^{-1} \amp \quad = \quad \pbr{\frac{x^{-3-1}}{y^2} } ^{-1}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad \pbr{\frac{x^{-4}}{y^2}}^{-1}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\quad = \quad \frac{y^2}{x^{-4}}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad y^2x^4</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
<em>There are other avenues to take to get to this answer. You could have distributed the <m>-1</m> power to the numerator and denominator first, for example.</em>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\dfrac{(x-y)^3}{x}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This cannot be simplified any further using exponent rules.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>(2x^2y^4)^3</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
(2x^2y^4)^3 \quad=\quad 2^3(x^2)^3(y^4)^3 \quad = \quad 8 x^{2\cdot 3} y^{4\cdot 3} \quad = \quad 8 x^6 y^{12}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m> \dfrac{x+y}{y}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This cannot be simplified further using exponent rules. Namely, be sure not to cancel the <m>y</m> from the numerator!
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>\pbr{\dfrac{\pi (x^{2})^6}{x^{2}y^{-4}}}^2</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>

<p>
<md>
<mrow>\pbr{\dfrac{\pi (x^{2})^6}{x^{2}y^{-4}}}^2 \amp \quad = \quad \pbr{\frac{\pi x^{12}}{x^2y^{-4}} } ^{2}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad \pbr{\frac{\pi x^{12-2}}{y^{-4}} } ^{2}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad \pbr{\frac{\pi x^{10}}{y^{-4}} } ^2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad \pbr{\pi x^{10}y^{4}} ^2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad \pi ^2 \cdot (x^{10})^2 \cdot (y^4)^2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad = \quad \pi ^2 x^{20} y^{8}</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
<em>There are other avenues to take to get to this answer. You could have distributed the <m>2</m> power to the numerator and denominator first, for example.</em>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m> \dfrac{x^2+y^3}{xy}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This cannot be simplified further using exponent rules.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



</worksheet>